WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.180
- I'm Kristen Dicerbo,

00:00:01.180 --> 00:00:03.040
the chief learning
officer at Khan Academy.

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.780
These are my five learning
science-backed tips

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:07.390
to remember what you studied.

00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:11.040
The key is to link it to
something you already know.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.790
Number one, make a to learn list.

00:00:13.790 --> 00:00:16.940
After reviewing your
materials, put them away

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:19.930
and make a list of all the
concepts you wanna learn.

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:23.170
Number two, explain each
concept to yourself.

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:27.380
Review what you understand in
as much detail as possible.

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:30.150
Number three, check what you missed.

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:32.980
Return to the lesson and
see if there's anything

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:34.550
you got wrong or forgot.

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:37.210
Number four, relate to other concepts.

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:40.070
For each new concept
ask how is this similar

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:41.510
to something I already know.

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:42.540
How is it different?

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:46.340
Number five, relate it
to your own experience.

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:49.334
Ask does this relate to
an experience I've had?

00:00:49.334 --> 00:00:50.167
How?

00:00:50.167 --> 00:00:52.940
Reviewing and relating information
to what you already know

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:54.970
helps store it in your long-term memory.

00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:57.963
Read the blog post below for
more learning science tips.

